## THE MOST IMPORTANT RULE: PRESERVE SPECIFICITY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Never collapse rich context into a generic title.

Example of what NOT to do:
  Conversation: "soap marketing → brand marketing → professional career"
  WRONG target_role: "Marketing Professional"
  CORRECT extraction:
    function = "Marketing"
    specialization = "Brand Marketing"
    industry = "Soap and personal care"
    career_intent = "professional"
    target_role = "Brand Marketing Strategist"

Each message in the conversation may add a layer of specificity.
RETAIN all layers — do not generalize or simplify.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
## FIELDS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

goal_summary:
  A complete narrative description of what the learner wants to achieve.
  Should incorporate all accumulated context: industry + function + specialization + career intent.
  Example: "Build a professional career in brand marketing within the soap and personal care industry"

target_role:
  The most specific, searchable job title constructable from all conversation context.
  COMPOSE it from function + specialization + industry context — do not just copy what the user said.
  Examples:
    - function=Marketing + specialization=Brand Marketing + industry=FMCG → "Brand Marketing Manager"
    - function=Cybersecurity + specialization=Penetration Testing → "Penetration Tester"
    - function=Software Engineering + specialization=Backend + industry=FinTech → "Backend Engineer"
  NEVER set to generic titles like "Marketing Professional", "Tech Person", "IT Worker"
  unless that is genuinely the most specific thing extractable from the entire conversation.

industry:
  The specific industry, market, or sector the learner wants to work in.
  Extract from any mention of products, companies, sectors, or markets.
  Examples: "Soap and personal care", "FMCG", "Healthcare", "FinTech", "Film and television",
            "Gaming", "EdTech", "Automotive"
  If no industry is mentioned, leave null.

function:
  The broad job function. Not a job title — the department or type of work.
  Examples: "Marketing", "Software Engineering", "Finance", "Human Resources",
            "Design", "Operations", "Sales", "Data Science", "Product Management"

specialization:
  The specific sub-area within the function. This is the key to preserving context.
  If the user mentions ANY specific area, capture it here.
  Examples:
    Marketing → "Brand Marketing", "Growth Marketing", "Content Marketing", "Digital Marketing"
    Software → "Backend Engineering", "Frontend Development", "DevOps", "Machine Learning"
    Finance → "Investment Banking", "Financial Analysis", "Risk Management"
  Combine multiple mentions: "brand marketing and marketing strategy" → "Brand Marketing and Strategy"

career_intent:
  'professional' → user wants a job, career, or income from this
  'personal'     → user wants it as a hobby or personal interest
  'unclear'      → not yet stated

role_confidence:
  'high'   → function + specialization + career_intent all identified from the conversation
  'medium' → function and career_intent known, but specialization unclear
  'low'    → only broad domain or industry known

goal_type:
  Classify the user's primary goal type. MUST be one of:
  - "career": User wants to get a job or build a professional career in this field (e.g. "become a cybersecurity engineer", "chartered accountant").
  - "academic": User is studying for school, university, or an exam (e.g. "learn linear algebra for an exam", "study for AP History").
  - "skill": User wants to acquire a specific practical skill (e.g. "learn Photoshop", "learn Python").
  - "hobby": User is learning strictly for fun, personal interest, or casual enjoyment (e.g. "learn photography for fun").
  - "unresolved": The goal type is completely unclear.

NOTE ON target_role:
  Only career goals strictly require a target_role. For academic, skill, or hobby goals, you may leave target_role null or adapt it loosely if appropriate, but do not force a non-career goal into a career role title.

experience_level:
  Map from any mention in the conversation:
  "nothing", "nil", "none", "no experience", "never", "beginner", "just starting" → "none"
  "courses", "tutorials", "self-taught", "hobby", "casual learning" → "casual"
  "workshop", "bootcamp", "training program" → "workshop"
  "personal projects", "side projects", "built apps", "academic projects" → "project"
  "professional", "job", "work", "employed", "industry experience" → "professional"

skills:
  Any skills the user mentions they already have. Include technical and soft skills.

interests:
  Stated learning preferences or aspects of the field that interest them.
  E.g. "prefer hands-on learning", "interested in consumer psychology", "likes data analysis"

learning_objectives:
  Why they want to learn. E.g. "get a job", "career change", "start a business",
  "freelance", "promotion", "personal interest"

contradictions:
  Genuine contradictions only. Not different phrasings of the same thing.

